</p>
<p>I don't think a recursive method is suitable for this kind of problem.
It requires having a complex strategy for exploring empty spaces.
For example, spiraling around the starting point.<br />
But this strategy comes up against the problem of the island hindering this progress, and which requires, once crossed, to carry out a new spiral advance to recover the points hidden during the previous spiral, but having to avoid the points already explored during the previous spiral.</p>
<p>You can also move forward by sectors from an initial point, but you will still encounter the same problem of the islet and its backtracking, which also multiply here with each roughness on the explored shores.</p>
<p>This requires complex calculations that are difficult to master and debug, not to mention the fact that recursive methods intensively use call stacks which it adds up for each new branch.</p>
<p>(Without neglecting the risk of going crazy, by striving to develop its recursive algorithms.)</p>
<p>The larger the grid and the more its recursive lists will conflict with each other, and more the computationnal time will be affected.</p>
<p>Of course there are already winning heuristics on this type of strategy, they are of a very high level, and we are here just on a minesweeper game where hundreds of lines of code have nothing to do.</p>
<p>My method only uses a single stack, its algorithm is easy to understand and requires a few lines of code.<br />
What more ?</p>

Q: How to get from a url string the path to a file with a FOR loop in a Windows batch file? A: <p>Your initial approach is correct in theory, but this script that you wrote probably fails because of delayed variable expansion, so to make a more efficient and less unpredictable script, you can use this approach I found:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>@echo off
setlocal enabledelayedexpansion enableextensions
setlocal

set /p &quot;url=Input URL to extract: &quot;

if &quot;%url%&quot; == &quot;&quot; ( exit /b 1 )

REM Check if the last character is a slash, if yes, remove it
if &quot;%url:~-1%&quot; == &quot;/&quot; (
    set &quot;url=%url:~0,-1%&quot;
) else (
    REM Uncomment this to avoid removing final slash, leaving the URL as it is
    REM goto :done
)

REM This string length technique was taken from the most
REM upvoted post here:
REM https://stackoverflow.com/questions/37345709/check-length-of-a-filepath-with-a-batch-file-stringlength
set /a len=1

REM Calculate string length to traverse it
for %%a in ( 4096 2048 1024 512 256 128 64 32 16 8 4 2 1 ) do (
    if not &quot;!url:~%%a!&quot; == &quot;&quot; ( set /a len+=%%a )
)

REM Once you have a length you can traverse the string
REM (subtract one for indexing purposes, otherwise the index overflows)

set /a len-=1
for /l %%a in (0,1,!len!) do (
    REM Check for slash
    if &quot;!url:~%%a,1!&quot; == &quot;/&quot; ( set &quot;substr=&quot; ) else (
        REM Append new characters
        set &quot;substr=!substr!!url:~%%a,1!&quot;
    )
)

REM Remove extracted substring
set &quot;url=!url:%substr%=!&quot;

:done
REM Uncomment this to remove final slash
REM set &quot;url=!url:~0,-1!&quot;

echo !url!
pause
endlocal
exit /b 0
</code></pre>
<p>If you are able to calculate the string length in advance, you can iterate through the entire <code>url</code> string with a <code>for /l</code> loop instead of some other technique.</p>
<p>This works because the <code>for /l</code> loop starts from the beginning of the <code>url</code>, and it adds every character until it finds a slash, so it clears the built-up <code>substr</code> variable. When the loop is done, the extracted substring variable is removed from the original <code>url</code> via the regular substring replacement in batch.</p>
<p>One last detail I want to note is that the script first checks if the last slash is also the last character in the <code>url</code> string, in which case it gets removed and the script processes the string up to the new final slash.</p>
<p>If you have any other questions or problems with this code let me know, happy to help :)</p>
